      "type": "text",
      "text": "{\"pair_id\": \"Werenro|450224\", \"verdict\": \"different_name\", \"reasoning\": \"The transliteration Jr.n-Rꜥw reads as the signs j, r, n, r, ꜥ, w. In Werenro, the sign r is matched by the first r, with the e as an extra vowel. The sign n is matched by n. The second r is matched by the second r. The sign ꜥ is matched by the final o, a vowel after r. The final w may be left out. The sign j may also be left out, but it cannot be represented by w. The initial W of Werenro comes before the first r, so it cannot stand for the final w of Rꜥw, which comes later in the order. It can only stand for j, and a w for a j is a wrong match. The W is therefore a consonant left over that the transliteration lacks, so the names differ.\"}"
